<!DOCTYPE html><html><head><title>IRC channel logs</title><style>html {
  background: #fdfdfd;
}

h1 {
  font-weight: 300;
}

h2 {
  font-weight: 200;
}

h3 {
  padding: .5em 0;
  border-top: 3px dotted #ddd;
  margin-bottom: 0;
}

form {
  width: 400px;
  display: flex;
}

input {
  width: 100%;
  display: flex;
  border-radius: .25em 0 0 .25em;
  border: 1px solid #aaa;
  border-right: 0;
  padding: 0.5em;
}

button {
  display: flex;
  border-radius: 0 .25em .25em 0;
  background-color: #007bff;
  border: 1px solid #007bff;
  padding: .5em;
  cursor: pointer;
  color: white;
}

button:hover {
  background-color: #0069d9;
  border-color: #0062cc;
}

a {
  color: #007bff;
  text-decoration: none;
}

a:hover {
  text-decoration: underline;
}

h4 {
  margin-bottom: .5em;
}

table td {
  padding: 0.75em;
}

table tr:hover {
  background: #eee;
}

.year {
  display: table;
}

.month {
  display: table-cell;
  padding-right: 1em;
}

ul {
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  list-style: none;
}

.nick {
  padding-right: 0.6rem;
  font-weight: bold;
  text-align: right;
  width: 13rem;
  display: table-cell;
}

.nick a {
  color: inherit;
  text-decoration: none;
}

.message {
  display: table-cell;
  padding-left: 0.6rem;
  border-left: 2px solid #333;
}

.notice {
  color: #859900;
  font-style: italic;
}

.line {
  line-height: 1.8rem;
  display: table;
}

#logs {
  margin-top: 1.5rem;
  padding: 1.5rem;
}
</style></head><body><h1>IRC channel logs</h1><h2>2024-12-13.log</h2><p><a href="/bootstrappable">back to list of logs</a></p><div id="logs"><div class="line" id="001113"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#001113" label="[00:11:13]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">been away for a few days, catching up... stikonas &quot;oh nice, looks like Gentoo is looking into taking mrustc -&gt; rustc bootstrap chain into its main repo&quot; --- ooh, where was that discussed?</span></div><div class="line" id="001126"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#001126" label="[00:11:26]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">fossy: #gentoo-rust</span></div><div class="line" id="001139"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#001139" label="[00:11:39]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">:)</span></div><div class="line" id="001234"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#001234" label="[00:12:34]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">fossy: there isn't that much code yet, but there are some WIP commits here: <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/Kangie/gentoo/tree/rustc-bootstrap">https://github.com/Kangie/gentoo/tree/rustc-bootstrap</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="001246"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#001246" label="[00:12:46]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">slightly different approach than what I had in my gentoo-overlay</span></div><div class="line" id="001434"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#001434" label="[00:14:34]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i see, very cool</span></div><div class="line" id="054453"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#054453" label="[05:44:53]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">interesting, microhs by itself requires only 8 extensions to be built: BangPatterns, CPP, FlexibleContexts, FlexibleInstances, FunctionalDependencies, MultiParamTypeClasses, ScopedTypeVariables and TypeSynonymInstances; and the only library it depends on is base package, which is available in different implementations</span></div><div class="line" id="054622"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#054622" label="[05:46:22]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it has it's own incomplete base package necessary to bootstrap itself, but it requires 20 extensions, so it's best to stick with base provided by hugs and nhc98</span></div><div class="line" id="054801"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#054801" label="[05:48:01]">&lt;unmush&gt;</a></span><span class="message">how good can a language be if it requires tons of extensions</span></div><div class="line" id="055154"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#055154" label="[05:51:54]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, I'd say so complex it's very difficult to understand code using those extensions</span></div><div class="line" id="055344"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#055344" label="[05:53:44]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and in way there is assumption that programmers are mathematicians that read hundreds of research papers</span></div><div class="line" id="055947"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#055947" label="[05:59:47]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">also base package depends on internals of implementation, so it makes no sense to try to build mhs's base before building mhs, so as far as I am concerned mhs requires only 8 extensions</span></div><div class="line" id="080100"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#080100" label="[08:01:00]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">meanwhile according to declaration in .cabal file for ghc, compiling compiler alone requires over 70 extensions, so having only 8 as required in mhs while gaining over 40 is a big step forward</span></div><div class="line" id="084303"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#084303" label="[08:43:03]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i see, the patched linux-2.4/tcc kernel defines ~270 syscalls, while musl-libc defines 433 already</span></div><div class="line" id="084331"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#084331" label="[08:43:31]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">surprisingly most user-space components do work regardless</span></div><div class="line" id="084536"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#084536" label="[08:45:36]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">time64 could become troublesome, since a notable amount got introduced for this</span></div><div class="line" id="084601"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#084601" label="[08:46:01]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">anyway, there's a little time remaining until year2038</span></div><div class="line" id="092242"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#092242" label="[09:22:42]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">one victim of this: vis-editor relying upon some POSIX 2001, which linux-2.4 doesn't fully cover</span></div><div class="line" id="092420"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#092420" label="[09:24:20]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">although oksh is mostly stable, i was hitting a quirk with it inside tty_ioctl (busybox-1.2.2.1 doesn't)</span></div><div class="line" id="092541"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#092541" label="[09:25:41]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">gdb was picking up musl headers and pipe2() seems to rely upon some missing syscall</span></div><div class="line" id="092624"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#092624" label="[09:26:24]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">most of this probably can be fixed or circuumvented; i'll see to such iregularities are detected compile-time instead of run-time</span></div><div class="line" id="100344"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#100344" label="[10:03:44]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ok, musl is posix-2011, linux-2.x didn't fully cover posix-2001 already, fiwix even less (which is desireable btw.)</span></div><div class="line" id="100434"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#100434" label="[10:04:34]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i'll just wipe musl syscall.h to remove what linux-2.x hasn't got</span></div><div class="line" id="100550"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#100550" label="[10:05:50]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">since i worked with vis-editor, i'll have to either re-write this to avoid pselect() or replace it with another vi clone entirely</span></div><div class="line" id="100808"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#100808" label="[10:08:08]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">fedora-core 7 ships a source-DVD and i may pull some stuff from there knowing this should match linux-2.x abi</span></div><div class="line" id="100931"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#100931" label="[10:09:31]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">strace already works, and gdb-7.12 probably will too soon (if it's autoconf tests against sanitized musl-libc headers)</span></div><div class="line" id="214401"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#214401" label="[21:44:01]">&lt;temp64&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Hey, are there any idiot-proof instructions on how to get something like LFS going starting from tcc?</span></div><div class="line" id="214555"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#214555" label="[21:45:55]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">temp64: yes</span></div><div class="line" id="214607"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#214607" label="[21:46:07]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message"> <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/fosslinux/live-bootstrap/">https://github.com/fosslinux/live-bootstrap/</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="214636"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#214636" label="[21:46:36]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">in fact it starts from far smaller binaries and only reaches tcc after a number of steps</span></div><div class="line" id="214726"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#214726" label="[21:47:26]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">hex0 assembler is 189 bytes I think and there is kaem-optional-shell (trivial non-interactive shell) which is about 600 bytes</span></div><div class="line" id="214741"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#214741" label="[21:47:41]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">this is also a useful read <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/fosslinux/live-bootstrap/blob/master/parts.rst">https://github.com/fosslinux/live-bootstrap/blob/master/parts.rst</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="215236"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#215236" label="[21:52:36]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it's high time bootstrappable.org mentioned live-bootstrap...</span></div><div class="line" id="215724"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#215724" label="[21:57:24]">&lt;temp64&gt;</a></span><span class="message">whoa, that's a lot of steps. I was wondering how hard it would be to run Podman or Docker, start `FROM scratch` and build a functional rootfs that way, without relying on tools like Yocto or Buildroot</span></div><div class="line" id="215730"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#215730" label="[21:57:30]">&lt;temp64&gt;</a></span><span class="message">there's a lot more work to it than I thought</span></div><div class="line" id="220002"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#220002" label="[22:00:02]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Well docker is at least as much a tool as yocto or buildroot, if not even more enclosing.</span></div><div class="line" id="220721"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#220721" label="[22:07:21]">&lt;temp64&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Yeah, but at least it gives me control over what goes into the final image. I have no idea how to remove packages from Buildroot and Yocto's core-image-minimal builds X11 libraries, which is insane</span></div><div class="line" id="220816"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#220816" label="[22:08:16]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">temp64: well, the amount of steps there is high because we try to rebuild everything, i.e. no pre-generated files are used...</span></div><div class="line" id="220842"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#220842" label="[22:08:42]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">e.g. we don't run any configure scripts before gnu autotools is bootstrapped, etc...</span></div><div class="line" id="220918"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#220918" label="[22:09:18]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">if you just want to go from tcc to gcc in a &quot;normal distro&quot; way, it's far fewer steps...</span></div><div class="line" id="221032"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#221032" label="[22:10:32]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">you still need libc, some tools such as coreutils, grep, sed, possibly findutils, binutils and probably a few more but not that much more</span></div><div class="line" id="221212"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#221212" label="[22:12:12]">&lt;temp64&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so a statically linked tcc would not be enough to build everything else?</span></div><div class="line" id="221223"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#221223" label="[22:12:23]">&lt;temp64&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I mean, as a starting point</span></div><div class="line" id="221320"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#221320" label="[22:13:20]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">in principle it will</span></div><div class="line" id="221332"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#221332" label="[22:13:32]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I should work but extra steps compared to what I remember of LFS</span></div><div class="line" id="221348"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#221348" label="[22:13:48]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, if you just have tcc binary, you'll first need to build some C library</span></div><div class="line" id="221356"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#221356" label="[22:13:56]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">the question is how do you build it</span></div><div class="line" id="221415"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#221415" label="[22:14:15]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">you don't have make or shell or anything like that</span></div><div class="line" id="221426"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#221426" label="[22:14:26]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Which is why I grabbed both musl and tcc for my initrd ^^</span></div><div class="line" id="221646"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#221646" label="[22:16:46]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">But you can compile a shell from just tcc, at least loksh is doable in a single command. (I pre-generate it here, didn't want to bother too much with C: <a rel="nofollow" href="https://hacktivis.me/git/bootstrap-initrd/file/make-root.sh.html#l68">https://hacktivis.me/git/bootstrap-initrd/file/make-root.sh.html#l68</a>  )</span></div><div class="line" id="221647"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#221647" label="[22:16:47]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, you can always manually include relevant C files from libc into your tcc command line</span></div><div class="line" id="221734"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#221734" label="[22:17:34]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(And I have tcc jit compile the init, regular C Compilers can't do this)</span></div><div class="line" id="221824"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#221824" label="[22:18:24]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">bootstrapping system generally involves parallel bootstrap track of a few components: compiler, libc, shell (+ various other tools) and perhaps kernel as well.</span></div><div class="line" id="221858"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#221858" label="[22:18:58]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so if you do all of them together, by the time you reach tcc, you have quite a few other helpful things</span></div><div class="line" id="222312"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#222312" label="[22:23:12]">&lt;temp64&gt;</a></span><span class="message">lanodan: you JIT-compile your system's init from initrd?</span></div><div class="line" id="222354"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#222354" label="[22:23:54]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">temp64: you don't need init system for bootstrapping</span></div><div class="line" id="222426"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#222426" label="[22:24:26]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">init system, e.g. systemd can be compiled well after GCC was bootstrapped</span></div><div class="line" id="222429"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#222429" label="[22:24:29]">&lt;temp64&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yes, I've read the README and I think I'll go from the chroot method but the initrd remark caught my attention</span></div><div class="line" id="222454"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#222454" label="[22:24:54]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, initrd way is basically put the same chroot stuff into initrd</span></div><div class="line" id="222501"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#222501" label="[22:25:01]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and let preexisting linux kernel run it</span></div><div class="line" id="222502"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#222502" label="[22:25:02]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Yeah, tcc allows to have #! just like scripts at the start, so the initial &quot;script&quot; is a C file. <a rel="nofollow" href="https://hacktivis.me/git/bootstrap-initrd/file/init.c.html">https://hacktivis.me/git/bootstrap-initrd/file/init.c.html</a>  (make-root.sh adds #!/usr/bin/tcc -run at the start of init.c)</span></div><div class="line" id="222503"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#222503" label="[22:25:03]">&lt;temp64&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oh</span></div><div class="line" id="222538"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#222538" label="[22:25:38]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah, having &quot;C&quot; scripts with tcc is one way to build other things such as libc, I think lanodan is not the only one who has done it</span></div><div class="line" id="222613"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#222613" label="[22:26:13]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">gio had something similar</span></div><div class="line" id="222717"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#222717" label="[22:27:17]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message"> <a rel="nofollow" href="https://gitlab.com/giomasce/nbs">https://gitlab.com/giomasce/nbs</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="222734"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#222734" label="[22:27:34]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(parts of it, specifically bison bootstrap was incorporated into live-bootstrap)</span></div><div class="line" id="222736"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#222736" label="[22:27:36]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Well I grab an already compiled musl (with ditching libc.a, too big) but I think I might try bootstrapping musl from something like a statically linked tcc.</span></div><div class="line" id="222845"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#222845" label="[22:28:45]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but I guess you need to keep your first JIT'ed C script really simple</span></div><div class="line" id="222852"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#222852" label="[22:28:52]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">since you basically need to include parts of libc to run it</span></div><div class="line" id="222909"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#222909" label="[22:29:09]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">tcc wouldn't just know what to do with e.g. unlink function, it has to be included into your first command line</span></div><div class="line" id="223225"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#223225" label="[22:32:25]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Well at least for init.c I could change fprintf into write/writev so then it's just syscalls and setenv, then I have a shell so more advanced commands are possible.</span></div><div class="line" id="223601"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#223601" label="[22:36:01]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">anyway, it's doable and shouldn't be too hard</span></div><div class="line" id="233104"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#233104" label="[23:31:04]">&lt;temp64&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I'm getting lots of 400s when live-bootstrap tries to download <a rel="nofollow" href="https://git.savannah.gnu.org/cgit/gnulib.git/snapshot/gnulib-356a414e.tar.gz">https://git.savannah.gnu.org/cgit/gnulib.git/snapshot/gnulib-356a414e.tar.gz</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="233123"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#233123" label="[23:31:23]">&lt;temp64&gt;</a></span><span class="message">or gnulib in general</span></div><div class="line" id="233719"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#233719" label="[23:37:19]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">temp64: how about if you try bash script rather than python?</span></div><div class="line" id="233731"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#233731" label="[23:37:31]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">there is download-distfiles.sh</span></div><div class="line" id="233746"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#233746" label="[23:37:46]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">perhaps some kind of user agent based throttling...</span></div><div class="line" id="233749"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#233749" label="[23:37:49]">&lt;temp64&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it's from the bash script</span></div><div class="line" id="233755"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#233755" label="[23:37:55]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oh ok...</span></div><div class="line" id="233758"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#233758" label="[23:37:58]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">then vice versa?</span></div><div class="line" id="233805"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#233805" label="[23:38:05]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">what if you let rootfs.py to download distfiles</span></div><div class="line" id="233842"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#233842" label="[23:38:42]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">also, as a last resort they are also here <a rel="nofollow" href="https://files.bootstrapping.world/">https://files.bootstrapping.world/</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="233852"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#233852" label="[23:38:52]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I think oriansj uploaded them to his server</span></div><div class="line" id="234201"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#234201" label="[23:42:01]">&lt;temp64&gt;</a></span><span class="message">other packages from git.savannah.gnu.org download just fine so I'm not sure what's going on</span></div><div class="line" id="234219"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#234219" label="[23:42:19]">&lt;temp64&gt;</a></span><span class="message">the web page tells me &quot;Unsupported snapshot format: gnulib-356a414e.tar.gz&quot;</span></div><div class="line" id="234303"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#234303" label="[23:43:03]">&lt;temp64&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I'd assume Firefox and curl use different user agents</span></div><div class="line" id="234950"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#234950" label="[23:49:50]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">hmm, perhaps that's something new...</span></div><div class="line" id="234952"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#234952" label="[23:49:52]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">CC fossy</span></div><div class="line" id="235050"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#235050" label="[23:50:50]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">maybe they removed any option for git snapshots...</span></div><div class="line" id="235055"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#235055" label="[23:50:55]">&lt;temp64&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I'm getting 400 for <a rel="nofollow" href="http://git.savannah.gnu.org/cgit/coreutils.git/snapshot/coreutils-9.4.tar.xz">http://git.savannah.gnu.org/cgit/coreutils.git/snapshot/coreutils-9.4.tar.xz</a>  too and looking at GNU's cgit I think it should end with *.tar.gz</span></div><div class="line" id="235056"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#235056" label="[23:50:56]">&lt;temp64&gt;</a></span><span class="message"> <a rel="nofollow" href="http://git.savannah.gnu.org/cgit/coreutils.git/refs/">http://git.savannah.gnu.org/cgit/coreutils.git/refs/</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="235130"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#235130" label="[23:51:30]">&lt;temp64&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah, snapshots for gnulib seem to be gone, only commit IDs remain <a rel="nofollow" href="https://git.savannah.gnu.org/cgit/gnulib.git/refs/">https://git.savannah.gnu.org/cgit/gnulib.git/refs/</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="235148"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#235148" label="[23:51:48]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">if you go to random commit</span></div><div class="line" id="235154"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#235154" label="[23:51:54]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">there is no more gzip option...</span></div><div class="line" id="235159"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#235159" label="[23:51:59]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I mean download option <a rel="nofollow" href="https://git.savannah.gnu.org/cgit/gnulib.git/commit/">https://git.savannah.gnu.org/cgit/gnulib.git/commit/</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="235215"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#235215" label="[23:52:15]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">e.g. compare to some other random cgit: <a rel="nofollow" href="https://git.zx2c4.com/cgit/commit/?id=09d24d7cd0b7e85633f2f43808b12871bb209d69">https://git.zx2c4.com/cgit/commit/?id=09d24d7cd0b7e85633f2f43808b12871bb209d69</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="235226"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#235226" label="[23:52:26]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">annoying...</span></div><div class="line" id="235253"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#235253" label="[23:52:53]">&lt;temp64&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oh, so that's where the URL came from... didn't know cgit could do that</span></div><div class="line" id="235413"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#235413" label="[23:54:13]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and fossy wanted to rebuild all pre-generated files in GNU tarballs...</span></div><div class="line" id="235428"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#235428" label="[23:54:28]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">which in hindsight is a good decision (looks at xz)</span></div><div class="line" id="235528"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#235528" label="[23:55:28]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but gnulib is generally without releases and other gnu projects just include it in their release tarball (not in the same form as in git repo though)</span></div><div class="line" id="235608"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#235608" label="[23:56:08]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so we were basically reimporting various gnulib's again into other tarballs</span></div><br /></div></body></html>